  "cover_text": "Georgetown — Sally Long, a long-time resident of the bank's Washington office, said the bank's board has urged its accountants to switch to smaller international accounts and then to pump money into local currency and organize its own currencies to minimize Wall Street's influence over the banking community. The bank has also advised its major five banks and 172,000 customers about the idea of consolidating and including overhead savings accounts, she said, in one instance. The non-profit organization has also done business with around 200 non-U.S. companies, including AT&T, Bank of America and Goldman Sachs, including Root Capital. She said the bank's predecessor, a U.S. bank report card vendor, has rejected the Washington agreement.\n\n\"Our individual service providers are helping to provide a trustworthy access to market-leading exchange technologies in order for the mix of values they pride themselves on to sustain and expand and spread to other markets,\" Long said. \"Outside the U.S., this group has worked just as hard to feed the global banking community as the De",
